# Set up Uncoil with your coding agent

Paste the prompt below into Claude Code, Cursor or Codex, run inside your agent project. It installs the SDK, signs you in, adds one line of code and checks that a trace arrives. You do the one thing an agent cannot do for you: approve the sign-in in your browser.

## The prompt

```text
Add Uncoil tracing to this project so I can see why my AI agent fails. Work fast, keep the diff small, and stop to tell me if any step fails.

1. Detect the language and the model SDK (openai, @anthropic-ai/sdk, Vercel AI SDK, LangChain/LangGraph, or other).
2. Install the SDK with the project's own package manager: "@uncoil.dev/sdk" (JS/TS) or "uncoil-sdk" (Python).
3. Run: npx @uncoil.dev/sdk login
   Show me the web address and code it prints, then wait. I'll approve in the browser. It saves UNCOIL_API_KEY to .env.local. Never ask me to paste a key, never print it, and make sure .env.local is in .gitignore.
4. Add tracing at startup, once, server-side only:
   - openai / Anthropic SDK: import { init } from "@uncoil.dev/sdk"; init();
   - Vercel AI SDK: wrap the model with uncoilMiddleware()
   - LangChain/LangGraph: callbacks: [new UncoilLangGraphHandler()]
   - Python: follow https://www.uncoil.dev/docs/python
5. Run: npx @uncoil.dev/sdk doctor  (Python: uncoil-doctor). It must send a test trace. If it fails, fix what it says and run it again.
6. Run my agent once for real.
7. Reply in 3 lines: files changed, the project name, and this link: https://www.uncoil.dev/dashboard
```

## What happens when you run it

1. The agent reads your project and installs the Uncoil SDK.
2. It runs `npx @uncoil.dev/sdk login`. The terminal shows a link and a short code like `ABCD-EFGH`. Open the link, sign in or sign up, and pick the project this folder should send to. Nothing is typed into the terminal, so the agent never waits on input.
3. The command saves `UNCOIL_API_KEY` and `UNCOIL_PROJECT_ID` to `.env.local` in that folder and adds the file to `.gitignore` if needed. Each folder keeps its own project, so two repos can use two projects.
4. The agent adds `init()` (or the matching helper for your framework), runs `npx @uncoil.dev/sdk doctor`, and runs your agent once.
5. Your first trace appears in the project you picked.

No account means no key, and no key means no data: Uncoil refuses traces without a valid key from a signed-in account.

## Where to paste it

| Agent | Where |
| --- | --- |
| Claude Code | Start `claude` in your project folder and paste the prompt as your first message. |
| Cursor | Open the project, open Agent chat, choose Agent mode, and paste the prompt. |
| Codex | Run `codex` in your project folder (or open it in the Codex app) and paste the prompt. |

The agent may ask permission to run `npx`, install packages, and edit files. Approve those. If it asks for a key, tell it to run `npx @uncoil.dev/sdk login` instead.

## If something goes wrong

- **No link appears.** Run `npx @uncoil.dev/sdk login --no-browser` yourself and open the printed link on any device. The code expires after 10 minutes; run the command again for a new one.
- **Traces land in the wrong project.** Run `npx @uncoil.dev/sdk doctor`. It names the project the key belongs to. Run `npx @uncoil.dev/sdk login` in this folder to switch.
- **`UNCOIL_API_KEY is not set`.** Plain Node and Python do not read `.env.local` by themselves. Use `node --env-file=.env.local app.js` or python-dotenv.
- **Nothing shows up after a minute.** Follow [Troubleshoot your first trace](/docs/troubleshooting).

Using Claude Code? The [Uncoil plugin](/docs/claude-plugin) installs these steps as skills, so you do not need to paste the prompt.

Prefer to do it by hand? The [quickstart](/docs/quickstart) shows every step.
